# tools/ales_stereo_band_witness.rish — stereo_band: the band-pass carried into stereo, the same two cutoffs on both channels, each channel carrying its own cascade state — the third rung of the stereo EQ / filter class (ALES172).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES172.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-075923_fill-ales172-lotus-stereo-band.md
# Stands on: lotus/band.rye (ALES46 — the mono two-stage band-pass Bandpass run once per channel, its ToneError reused whole), lotus/tone.rye (ALES40, ALES45 — the high-pass and low-pass stages and the precheck sharing one
# coefficient-and-span validation), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip).
#
# lotus/stereo_band.rye isolates a band on a stereo master: it runs ALES46's proven mono band-pass on BOTH channels with the SAME two cutoffs — a high-pass at the low cutoff drops the lows, then a low-pass at the high cutoff
# drops the highs, keeping only the band between. A band-pass's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each
# channel band-passes its own content and, like the tone control and unlike the stateless shelf, keeps its own cascade state so the two channels never cross. It validates both cutoffs and the span once against the shared
# length before either channel is mutated (BadCoeff on an illegal cutoff, BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never band-passes one channel and leaves the other torn and no legal low cutoff advances a state before
# an illegal high cutoff refuses; ToneError reused whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo band-pass law (each channel equal to ALES46's mono Bandpass.run with the
# same two cutoffs byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the carry law (a span band-passed in two pieces with each channel carrying its own state equal to the whole once on both), the image law (an identical-channel
# master staying identical through the band-pass, a settled constant rejected to zero per channel), and the reset / balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_band_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-band: Language EN — stereo_band isolates a band on a stereo master by running ALES46's proven mono band-pass on both channels with the same two cutoffs: a high-pass at the low cutoff drops the lows, then a low-pass at the high cutoff drops the highs, keeping only the band between. A band-pass's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each channel band-passes its own content and, like the tone control and unlike the stateless shelf, keeps its own cascade state so the two channels never cross. It validates both cutoffs and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never band-passes one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-band: Style Radiant — ALES170 opened the stereo EQ and filter class with the one-pole tone control, and ALES171 followed with the two-band shelf; the gesture a keeper reaches for to isolate a range on a master — the telephone voice, the drum bus carved to its body, the wah's throat — is the band-pass: drop the lows below one cutoff, drop the highs above another, keep only the band between. ALES46 built that for a mono clip as a cascade: a high-pass at the low cutoff followed by a low-pass at the high cutoff, each stage carrying its own state so a span band-passed in two pieces equals the whole band-passed once. This rung carries it into stereo the way the EQ class asks — a stereo band-pass holding two mono band-passes, both built with the same four coefficients, run on each channel so the band turns on both speakers together. Like every parametric edit, a band-pass's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for free, with no linking and no measurement; each channel band-passes its own content, which is exactly right for equalization — an identical-channel, mono-in-stereo master stays identical because the same input through the same cutoffs evolves the same state, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. Like the tone control and unlike the stateless shelf, a band-pass carries running estimates, so each channel keeps its own cascade state; linking the state across channels would bleed one channel's transients into the other's band, a bug here exactly as it would be for the tone control. The crux is four laws: the stereo band-pass law (each channel equal to ALES46's mono band-pass with the same two cutoffs byte for byte, the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed); the carry law (a master band-passed in two pieces, each channel continuing its own ending cascade state, equal to the whole once on both, no fresh transient at the seam); the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the band-pass, a settled constant rejected to zero on both as the high-pass stage drops direct current); and the reset, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (reset re-opening both channels' transients, an all-silent master band-passing to silence on both, the lengths held, an illegal low cutoff, an illegal high cutoff, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels and all four states untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-band: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each band-passed through ALES46's own two-stage cascade, both channels' four cascade states run at full i64 precision so no fractional information is lost between samples; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a band-pass writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit), and the two inner band-passes are asserted to carry the same two cutoffs so the shared knobs hold the image; both cutoffs and the span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never band-passes one channel and leaves the other torn and no legal low cutoff advances a state before an illegal high cutoff refuses; StereoBandError is ALES46's ToneError (BadCoeff, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo band-pass law is proven by running both stereo_band and ALES46's mono band-pass on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the carry law proves a span band-passed in two pieces with each channel carrying its own state equals the whole once on both; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical through the band-pass and a settled constant is rejected to zero per channel; and the reset, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove reset clearing all four states and re-opening both transients, an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the three refusals leaving both channels and all four states untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the cutoffs over sample indices rather than crossovers in hertz, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_band"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_band.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-band: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_band.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-band: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-band: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-band' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-band: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-band line"
